    label_min_fan_cap_m3h:   'Min-vent fan capacity, m³/h',

    help_body_html:    '<h2 class="mt-0">📐 Ventilation Calculation Methodology</h2><p class="muted">Physics-based model: Qmin = max(CO₂, moisture) including burners · Qmax = heat balance capped by the ventilation mode. SI units.</p><h3>1 — Qmin: CO₂ balance (CIGR 1984)</h3><p>Biological minimum from live weight. Checked against the Aviagen 2018 rates within ~10 %.</p><div class="info-block formula">H_total = 10.62 × W^0.75 × N_eff   [W]<br>Q_CO₂ = H_total × 0.153 / 2.6 × 1.5   [m³/h]<br><span class="muted">W = body weight [kg] · N_eff = effective flock · 0.153 = CO₂ yield · 2.6‰ = limit · 1.5 = safety</span></div><h3>2 — Qmin: moisture balance (CIGR 2002, house level)</h3><p>Vapour from birds and litter (and open-flame burners). Total heat is corrected for temperature (+2 % per °C below 20 °C), latent = total − house-level sensible.</p><div class="info-block formula">f_T = (1000 + 20·(20 − T_in)) / 1000<br>Q_sens = [0.61·(1000 + 20·(20 − T_in)) − 0.228·T_in²] / 1000 × 10.62·W^0.75   [W]<br>Q_lat = 10.62·W^0.75 × f_T − Q_sens   [W]<br>G = Q_lat × 3600 / 2430 × N_eff  (+ V_gas × 1607)   [g/h]<br>Q_RH = G / (ρ_in × ΔW)  ≤  Q_ceiling   [m³/h]<br>ΔW = W_in,max − W_out   W = 621.945·Pv/(p − Pv)   Psat = 611.2·exp(17.67·T/(T+243.5))<br><span class="muted">The more humid the outside air, the more air is needed — up to the ceiling of the mode; at the ceiling the target RH cannot be held by ventilation (heat or dehumidify).</span></div><h3>3 — Qmin final</h3><p>Burner dilution air is added to the CO₂ branch, burner vapour to the moisture branch.</p><div class="info-block formula">Q_min = max(Q_CO₂ + Q_dilution, Q_RH)</div><h3>4 — Qmax: heat balance</h3><p>Air that removes the house-level sensible heat of the birds. UA is used only from a plausible calibration of this house (cooling-curve test ≥ 10 min, envelope U 0.1–8 W/(m²·K)).</p><div class="info-block formula">Q_max = (N_eff × Q_sens − UA × ΔT) × 3600 / (ρ_in × Cp × ΔT)  ≤  Q_ceiling<br>ΔT = T_in − T_out   ρ_in = 353.05/(T_in + 273.15) × p/101325   Cp = 1006 J/(kg·K)<br>p = 101325 × (1 − 2.25577·10⁻⁵ × Z)^5.2559   [Pa]   Z — farm altitude (ASHRAE 2017, eq. 3)<br>Q_ceiling = 4 × Q_CO₂ (age &lt; tunnel_min_day)  |  Q_tunnel (age ≥ tunnel_min_day)<br><span class="muted">Cold: UA×ΔT &gt; 0 → walls help cooling → Qmax ↓.   Hot: UA×ΔT &lt; 0 → walls add load → Qmax ↑.</span></div><h3>5 — Heater duty</h3><p>Solved together with the burners’ own dilution air, which must be heated too.</p><div class="info-block formula">P × d = UA×ΔT + ρ_in·Cp·ΔT·Q_min(d)/3600 − N_eff × Q_sens<br>Q_min(d) = max(Q_CO₂ + Q_dilution(d), Q_RH(d))<br><span class="muted">If the equation cannot be met at d = 100 %, the heaters are reported as insufficient.</span></div><h3>6 — Open-flame burners: CO₂ dilution</h3><p>1 m³ of gas → 1000 L of CO₂ → ~577 m³ of air to dilute it to 2.6‰ (≈ 40× the combustion air).</p><div class="info-block formula">V_gas = P_kW / 10 × d   [m³/h]<br>Q_dilution = V_gas × 1000 / 2.6 × 1.5   [m³/h]   (≈ 58 m³/(h·kW))<br><span class="muted">At ΔT = 30 K heating this air takes ≈ 58 % of the burner power.</span></div><h3>7 — Tunnel mode</h3><p>Tunnel only when the heat balance needs more than half of the tunnel flow — side-wall transitional ventilation covers 40–50 % (Ross 2025). Air speed limited by age (Cobb 2021). Qmin is unchanged.</p><div class="info-block formula">Q_max = Q_tunnel  ⇐  age ≥ tunnel_min_day  ∧  T_out ≥ tunnel_min_temp  ∧  Q_heat &gt; 0.5 × Q_tunnel<br>Q_tunnel = v × S × 3600   v ≤ 0.3 (age &lt; 5) · 0.5 (age &lt; 14) · 1.8 m/s (age &lt; 21)<br>ΔT_rise = N × Q_sens / (ρ × Cp × Q)   T_fan end = T_inlet + ΔT_rise<br>Fans:  N·(q₀ − s·P) = Q   P = ρ/2·(1/S² + 1/(A_in·Cd)²)·Q² + ΔP_pad(Q/A_in)   s = (q₀ − q₂₅)/25 Pa<br>Tunnel sufficient:  v ≥ v_target(age)  ∧  T_eff(fan end) &lt; 30 °C (Cobb 2021 §9.7);  T_eff &gt; T_profile(day) → watch the birds<br><span class="muted">Tunnel tool: comfort is judged at the fan end, where the air is warmer. Wind cooling: table up to 29.4 °C, constant to 35 °C (UGA), fades to zero at 37.8 °C — bird surface temperature. Rise &gt; 2.8 °C between ends is flagged (Cobb).</span></div><h3>8 — Mortality correction</h3><p>Cycle mortality spread evenly over the days.</p><div class="info-block formula">S(day) = (1 − m_cycle)^((day − 1)/(cycle_days − 1))<br>N_eff = N × S(day)</div><h3>9 — Evaporative pad cooling</h3><p>The limit is the wet-bulb temperature of the outside air. Pad efficiency η and pressure drop come from the manufacturer curves (SANHE) by pad type, thickness and actual velocity — values for new pads; UGA field data: 70–75 %.</p><div class="info-block formula">W(T_wb) = [(2501 − 2.326·T_wb)·W_s(T_wb) − 1.006·(T − T_wb)] / (2501 + 1.86·T − 4.186·T_wb)  →  T_wb<br>T_pad = T_db − η × (T_db − T_wb)<br><span class="muted">T_wb is solved from the ASHRAE psychrometric equation (Fundamentals 2017, eq. 33), not Stull’s fit, which was off by up to +1 °C in hot dry air. Sufficiency is judged at the fan end: the cooled air warms over the birds.</span></div><h3>9.1 — Air velocity through the pad</h3><p>With the 25 Pa rating the airflow is taken at the operating point including the pad resistance; otherwise the 0 Pa rating is used (overstated).</p><div class="info-block formula">v = Q / (pad_length × pad_height)<br>Q = Q₀ − s × ΔP_pad(v)   s = (Q₀ − Q₂₅)/25 Pa<br>A_rec = Q / v_design   v_design = 1.14 m/s (100 mm) · 1.78 m/s (150 mm)<br><span class="muted">Allowed range 0.6–2.5 m/s. Too high → water carried into the house · too low → mould risk.</span></div><h3>9.2 — Water consumption</h3><p>Psychrometric mass balance.</p><div class="info-block formula">ΔW = η × (W_sat(T_wb) − W_out)   [kg/kg]<br>Q_water = Q/3600 × ρ_da(T_pad, W_out + ΔW) × ΔW × 60   [L/min]<br>ρ_da = p / (287.042 · T_K · (1 + 1.607858·W))   [kg dry air / m³]<br><span class="muted">The fans move the cooled, humidified air, so the dry-air mass is taken at the pad outlet. Check: UGA water chart, 90 °F / 60 % / 100,000 cfm, 6 in pad → 1.75 gpm; here 1.76 gpm at η = 0.75.</span></div><h3>9.3 — Water supply temperature</h3><p>Water colder than the dew point of the incoming air condenses vapour on the pad and lowers saturation — flagged. No correction of the pad outlet temperature is applied.</p><div class="info-block formula">T_water &lt; T_dew(T_out, RH_out)  →  ⚠</div><hr class="section-divider"><p class="muted fs-12">References: CIGR (1984, 2002) · Aarnink, CIGR Journal · Ross Broiler Handbook (2025) · Cobb Broiler Management Guide (2021) · UGA Poultry Housing Tips (Czarick) · University of Kentucky AFS ch. 9 · ASHRAE Fundamentals (2017) · SANHE pad data</p>',
